tests/z80corpus/ -- .COM files tests/z80test.c reads.  Test input, nothing else.

PIP.COM, DUMP.COM, SUBMIT.COM and SAVE.COM are Digital Research's own CP/M 3
programs, unmodified, from the cpm3bin drop of the Unofficial CP/M Web Site
(cpm3bin.zip, "CP/M 3 BINARY w/YEAR 2000 fixes").  Licensed by the 9 July 2022
grant from Bryan Sparks (President, DRDOS, Inc.) at
<http://cpm.z80.de/license.html>, which carries no distribution restriction and
which cpm.z80.de states covers the whole archive.

They are 8080 code.  Nothing on this machine executes them in the ordinary way
and nothing stages them onto a disk image: our utilities are native Z8000
programs -- src/cmd/pip.c and src/cmd/stat.c are DRI's sources rebuilt as .Z8K,
and they beat anything a shim can interpret.  The CP/M-80 shim exists so that
FOREIGN software runs here, not as a way of acquiring a PIP we already have.

Each of the four answers a different question:

  PIP.COM     8,632 B  the gate.  A file copy is a known-answer test: the
                       verdict is a byte-for-byte cmp, which is the only kind
                       of verdict that catches a silently short copy.
  DUMP.COM      960 B  first light.  The smallest DRI program that opens a
                       file, reads it and prints something checkable.
  SUBMIT.COM  5,376 B  a GENCOM-bound .COM: first byte 0xC9, one RSX.  The
                       loader has to refuse this by name rather than execute
                       the header record's RET and exit looking successful.
  SAVE.COM    1,792 B  an RSX-ONLY file: 0xC9 at the image base too, so there
                       is no program in it at all.  loader3.asm:253 is the
                       code that exists to tell these apart, and this is the
                       file that exercises the distinction.  It is also the
                       one program in the corpus whose module is ENTERED --
                       by the second function 59 a CCP asks an RSX-only
                       command with -- so it is what says the chain works
                       and not merely that it was built.
